= Preface <preface>

This document is a compilation of texts on Italian rapier fencing.
Some were originally handouts, others documentation, yet others a way for me to work through a topic; most were written in preparation for classes taught at _Klingenspiel_#index[Klingenspiel] in Vienna, others are my own training notes.
All of them are living documents---each time I return to a topic, I update that chapter with new drills, ideas, and notes.
For now, the material here is confined to 'rapier alone', as that is my primary focus.
Once I start working more intensively on rapier and dagger, that will begin to appear here too.

===== Style
#index-start("Style")
A fencer reading through this document will hopefully get the impression that, while it is not confined to the teachings of a particular fencing master, the approach described is stylistically coherent.
I do not yet have a succinct definition of that style, but I do have some bullet points.
I am looking for a style of Italian rapier fencing that:

- is at least as 'martial' as it is 'art'.
- is distinctly 'rapier', not 'heavy smallsword' or 'long sabre'.
- is based on a set of principles that are simultaneously derived from the Italian treatises and logically sound.
- has viable defensive and offensive options.
- treats the rapier as a 'thrust-and-cut' weapon the same ways as a sabre is a 'cut-and-thrust' weapon.
- integrates footwork, bladework, and body movement.
- is not linear.
- considers the empty hand (the 'other strong hand') as an integral component of every action.
- not averse to using all parts of the rapier in offence and defence (pommel, quillons, etc.).

I am not really a tournament fencer, and so my criteria for integrating something into my fencing is that I can make it work in hard sparring, that is, against an opponent who is really trying to win.
#index-end("Style")

===== Using the Document
Although most chapters reference each other in some way,#footnote[These cross-references are green and clickable in the PDF.] all are written to work as stand-alone documents.
The last chapter, @cues-and-details, contains the central ideas from the preceding chapters, condensed into coaching cues or descriptions of detail.
This is meant both as a quick reference for someone already familiar with the material, and as a starting point for someone looking for a particular topic, but wanting more detail than the index provides.
The chapters on @balance-and-voiding and @empty-hand-basics both contain progressive drills on a topic.

The most recent version of this document, along with the source code and sections that are still in progress, can be found at #link("https://github.com/laugedideriksen/ItalianRapier").
